  "abstract": "Jensen's criterion says that RH holds iff every generalized Laguerre expression $\\mathcal L_p[\\Xi](t)=[y^{2p}]\\,\\Xi(t+iy)\\Xi(t-iy)$ is nonnegative. Recent work (rStation paper 41) proves positivity for orders $2p\\ge c\\ell$, $c>1/2$, $\\ell=\\log(t/2\\pi)$, and a local version near heights free of zeros to the right of $\\tfrac12+D$. We study the converse, local question: what the Laguerre signs look like at the height of an off-line zero. All results are unconditional, and §§2–5 hold verbatim for the Davenport–Heilbronn function, so none of this approaches RH. (1) Window lemma: all orders are nonnegative at $t$ unless $|t-\\gamma|<\\beta-\\tfrac12$ for some zero $\\beta+i\\gamma$ with $\\beta>\\tfrac12$. Combined with the Balasubramanian–Conrey–Heath-Brown mean value and Conrey's $\\theta=4/7$, all orders are simultaneously nonnegative on a proportion at least $0.839$ of $[T,2T]$. (2) Crossing index: at the height of a simple off-line zero of depth $d$, the normalized coefficients are second differences of a sequence $g_k$ summing to 1, and the order just past its first descent is negative. (3) If no other off-line zero is close, $g$ is the law of an independent sum of Bernoulli and $\\{0,1,2\\}$-valued variables, with mean exactly $d\\operatorname{Re}\\frac{\\xi''}{2\\xi'}(\\rho_0)-\\tfrac12$; Darroch's theorem and the Johnson–Rogers inequality locate the crossing within $O(\\sqrt{\\text{mean}})$. (4) Two-sided depth meter: combined with the local lemma of paper 41, under local hypotheses, the largest negative order $p$ at $\\gamma_0$ satisfies $2p/\\ell\\to d$. Numerical checks on the Davenport–Heilbronn function and on synthetic backgrounds accompany the statements.",
